WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.143 --> 00:00:01.841
- Good morning, Believe Nation, it's Evan.

00:00:01.841 --> 00:00:04.867
My one word is believe and I
believe that you have something

00:00:04.867 --> 00:00:06.950
special that when you unleash it,

00:00:06.950 --> 00:00:08.801
it will be able to change the world.

00:00:08.801 --> 00:00:10.975
So, to help you on your
journey, today's message is,

00:00:10.975 --> 00:00:13.361
"Master one thing."

00:00:13.361 --> 00:00:14.763
Over to you, Tony Robbins.

00:00:14.763 --> 00:00:17.194
(rooster crowing)

00:00:17.194 --> 00:00:19.710
♫ I wake up every mornin'

00:00:19.710 --> 00:00:22.442
♫ Espresso keep me goin'

00:00:22.442 --> 00:00:26.378
♫ I wake up every mornin'

00:00:26.378 --> 00:00:29.643
- Most people who start a
business very often will start

00:00:29.643 --> 00:00:31.121
two and three, four, or more,

00:00:31.121 --> 00:00:32.730
and the reason that they do
it is because the first one

00:00:32.730 --> 00:00:33.948
isn't succeeding or they no longer...

00:00:33.948 --> 00:00:34.781
- They lack patience.

00:00:34.781 --> 00:00:35.614
- Exactly right.

00:00:35.614 --> 00:00:37.392
And so, what happens to that person,

00:00:37.392 --> 00:00:38.913
is they're never going to
be successful in most cases,

00:00:38.913 --> 00:00:39.746
unless they get lucky.

00:00:39.746 --> 00:00:40.953
You get lucky and bounce across

00:00:40.953 --> 00:00:42.315
something that's easier to do.

00:00:42.315 --> 00:00:44.703
But most are always lookin'
for that next level.

00:00:44.703 --> 00:00:47.915
What my view is, is it's
great to test all these things

00:00:47.915 --> 00:00:50.103
but you got to find what is your flagship?

00:00:50.103 --> 00:00:52.632
What is it that you're going
to commit your soul to?

00:00:52.632 --> 00:00:54.826
Because if you don't do that,
the inevitable challenges

00:00:54.826 --> 00:00:56.568
are going to come up and you're
going to then move on to something

00:00:56.568 --> 00:00:57.945
else more enjoyable.
- 100 percent.

00:00:57.945 --> 00:00:59.799
- And so, the other thing I look at is,

00:00:59.799 --> 00:01:03.017
business is about constantly,
not only adding such massive

00:01:03.017 --> 00:01:04.721
value, doing more for
others than anybody else,

00:01:04.721 --> 00:01:07.770
but it's also, simultaneously,
about your own psychology,

00:01:07.770 --> 00:01:11.290
it's your ability to go
through thresholds of control.

00:01:11.290 --> 00:01:14.564
It's like, I can remember
when I didn't have $50,000

00:01:14.564 --> 00:01:16.117
to keep the doors open on my company

00:01:16.117 --> 00:01:18.637
and I have 12 employees,
and 11 wanted to quit 'cause

00:01:18.637 --> 00:01:20.850
they hated the person running the show.

00:01:20.850 --> 00:01:23.694
And $50,000 to me, is like,
$500 million to me today

00:01:23.694 --> 00:01:26.032
and I figured out how to
get through that threshold.

00:01:26.032 --> 00:01:27.933
And once I did, like,
all the problems related

00:01:27.933 --> 00:01:29.006
to that were handled.

00:01:29.006 --> 00:01:30.125
I know you've done this as well.

00:01:30.125 --> 00:01:32.671
And then I, you know, I
had a $5 million lawsuit,

00:01:32.671 --> 00:01:35.965
it was totally unfair and unjust,
and I finally had to just,

00:01:35.965 --> 00:01:37.590
the amount of time, and energy, and money,

00:01:37.590 --> 00:01:38.813
I had to just bite the bullet and do it.

00:01:38.813 --> 00:01:39.790
And $5 million was more than I'd ever met.

00:01:39.790 --> 00:01:43.206
Then I had a partner, who
took a company that was losing

00:01:43.206 --> 00:01:45.972
a million dollars a day and
turned it to $1.5 billion

00:01:45.972 --> 00:01:50.204
in positive Ebitda, I'm not
mentioning names, Amway.

00:01:50.204 --> 00:01:51.704
And, he wanted me to join him in business

00:01:51.704 --> 00:01:54.786
with some other partners, not
doing the multi-level side,

00:01:54.786 --> 00:01:57.138
did the business, put in 10 million bucks,

00:01:57.138 --> 00:01:59.475
we all put in $10 million,
there's $40 million in that.

00:01:59.475 --> 00:02:01.472
But I signed joint and several,
which I didn't understand

00:02:01.472 --> 00:02:03.364
in those days what that meant.

00:02:03.364 --> 00:02:05.516
And two of my partners were
supposedly billionaires,

00:02:05.516 --> 00:02:06.791
and they went broke.

00:02:06.791 --> 00:02:10.565
We bought some more companies,
I ended up with $120 million

00:02:10.565 --> 00:02:12.524
in debt that I was on the hook for,

00:02:12.524 --> 00:02:14.046
no one else had any money.

00:02:14.046 --> 00:02:16.486
And I'm getting up to do a
seminar wanting to throw up.

00:02:16.486 --> 00:02:17.883
That was a new threshold of control.

00:02:17.883 --> 00:02:19.912
So, when you know, when
you ski or you snowboard,

00:02:19.912 --> 00:02:22.581
and you think, you're just new at it,

00:02:22.581 --> 00:02:24.334
and you think you're
going down a blue or green

00:02:24.334 --> 00:02:25.700
and it turns out to be a double black?

00:02:25.700 --> 00:02:26.533
(laughs)

00:02:26.533 --> 00:02:28.229
And then you're like, "What the fuck?"

00:02:28.229 --> 00:02:30.767
And so, you have two choices,
one is you start to go down

00:02:30.767 --> 00:02:31.937
and you freak out and you're going to die,

00:02:31.937 --> 00:02:33.540
you slam down on the ground
and you try to hang on

00:02:33.540 --> 00:02:34.373
for dear life.

00:02:34.373 --> 00:02:36.372
Or, the other is, baby, you
focus on where you want to go

00:02:36.372 --> 00:02:37.972
and find a way to curve.
- And go.

00:02:37.972 --> 00:02:39.386
- You find a way to cut.

00:02:39.386 --> 00:02:41.688
And once you do it a couple of times...

00:02:41.688 --> 00:02:42.521
- You get used to it.

00:02:42.521 --> 00:02:43.779
- You have no more fear of that element.

00:02:43.779 --> 00:02:47.126
Now my biggest one, out of
that, that led me to a billion.

00:02:47.126 --> 00:02:49.016
I have 31 companies now
to give you an idea.

00:02:49.016 --> 00:02:52.732
Seven different industries
as diverse as, like,

00:02:52.732 --> 00:02:55.099
you know, stem cells to virtual reality.

00:02:55.099 --> 00:02:57.127
We have the exclusive to the
NBA now in virtual reality

00:02:57.127 --> 00:02:58.768
to give you an example.

00:02:58.768 --> 00:03:02.285
I got 1200 employees,
we're on three continents,

00:03:02.285 --> 00:03:05.098
and we've got $5 billion in sales.

00:03:05.098 --> 00:03:07.751
But I did that because I first
stayed on one frickin' thing.

00:03:07.751 --> 00:03:08.584
- That's exactly right.

00:03:08.584 --> 00:03:09.897
- And I got so masterful at it.

00:03:09.897 --> 00:03:11.564
- So one of the messages
that I've repeated over

00:03:11.564 --> 00:03:12.898
and over and over again in this channel

00:03:12.898 --> 00:03:14.938
because it's so important,
is the number one rule

00:03:14.938 --> 00:03:16.296
about making money.

00:03:16.296 --> 00:03:17.347
Which is, if you want to make money,

00:03:17.347 --> 00:03:19.224
you have to provide value.

00:03:19.224 --> 00:03:20.786
It's the number one
rule about making money.

00:03:20.786 --> 00:03:23.103
The more value you can provide somebody,

00:03:23.103 --> 00:03:25.837
a client who's facing a
problem of frustration,

00:03:25.837 --> 00:03:27.420
the more money you're
going to be able to make,

00:03:27.420 --> 00:03:28.798
the more money you're
going to be able to charge.

00:03:28.798 --> 00:03:31.761
If you want to make money
quickly, provide value quickly.

00:03:31.761 --> 00:03:33.192
That's how it works.

00:03:33.192 --> 00:03:35.590
The challenge is being
able to provide the value.

00:03:35.590 --> 00:03:37.409
'Cause when you first
start off in business,

00:03:37.409 --> 00:03:40.791
you don't know enough to
provide amazing value.

00:03:40.791 --> 00:03:44.228
You can provide some value,
you can help people out,

00:03:44.228 --> 00:03:45.712
but you can't provide enough value

00:03:45.712 --> 00:03:47.924
to charge a premium price, yet.

00:03:47.924 --> 00:03:49.888
And that comes through mastery.

00:03:49.888 --> 00:03:51.955
That comes from understanding
the thing that you want

00:03:51.955 --> 00:03:53.889
to be amazing at, that you
want to be world-class at,

00:03:53.889 --> 00:03:56.312
that you want to be the
Tony Robbins of, at.

00:03:56.312 --> 00:03:57.597
What is that thing?

00:03:57.597 --> 00:03:59.935
And go all in it and focus
on it and get better,

00:03:59.935 --> 00:04:01.995
and get better, and get
better, and get better.

00:04:01.995 --> 00:04:05.553
You can get better quickly if
you focus and practice on it.

00:04:05.553 --> 00:04:08.169
You can outpace your
competition really quickly,

00:04:08.169 --> 00:04:09.882
like I did at my agency,

00:04:09.882 --> 00:04:12.055
because I was making
multiple videos a day,

00:04:12.055 --> 00:04:14.219
where they were making one video a month,

00:04:14.219 --> 00:04:15.060
or one video a week,

00:04:15.060 --> 00:04:17.122
and having a really hard time with that.

00:04:17.122 --> 00:04:19.907
You can get better really
quickly with dedicated,

00:04:19.907 --> 00:04:21.641
focused, effort on it.

00:04:21.641 --> 00:04:23.368
But most people don't do that.

00:04:23.368 --> 00:04:25.616
Too many entrepreneurs
are super scattered.

00:04:25.616 --> 00:04:27.831
And it's extra hard because,
maybe, you have a full-time job

00:04:27.831 --> 00:04:29.386
you haven't quit yet, you've got family

00:04:29.386 --> 00:04:31.072
and other commitments.

00:04:31.072 --> 00:04:33.054
Plus, you have 100 ideas in your head,

00:04:33.054 --> 00:04:34.549
and so, you're starting
this, and doing this,

00:04:34.549 --> 00:04:36.203
and starting that, and doing this.

00:04:36.203 --> 00:04:39.147
And then, if I look at your
productivity of actually moving

00:04:39.147 --> 00:04:41.836
forward on that thing that
you have the ability to be

00:04:41.836 --> 00:04:45.416
Tony Robbins level at, how much
time are you really spending

00:04:45.416 --> 00:04:47.241
on that one thing?

00:04:47.241 --> 00:04:48.974
Will it be like a start,
and a stop, and a start,

00:04:48.974 --> 00:04:49.807
and a stop.

00:04:49.807 --> 00:04:51.451
And, maybe, on Monday and then, pull back,

00:04:51.451 --> 00:04:53.964
and then, maybe again on
Friday, and then pull back,

00:04:53.964 --> 00:04:56.122
you never put in enough
actual work on that thing

00:04:56.122 --> 00:04:57.982
to become the Tony Robbins.

00:04:57.982 --> 00:04:59.944
To become world-class at that thing.

00:04:59.944 --> 00:05:01.936
And that's where you're going
to provide lots of value

00:05:01.936 --> 00:05:03.269
and that's where you're
going to make lots of money.

00:05:03.269 --> 00:05:05.605
That's what you need
to get really good at,

00:05:05.605 --> 00:05:08.356
otherwise you're going to constantly
play in this average zone.

00:05:08.356 --> 00:05:10.721
If you look at this channel,
you look at the Top 10 series

00:05:10.721 --> 00:05:14.184
that you guys love, and is
what, you know, we're known for,

00:05:14.184 --> 00:05:15.612
there's lots of people that
are trying to copy that.

00:05:15.612 --> 00:05:16.512
There's lots of people who
are tryin' to make their

00:05:16.512 --> 00:05:19.194
own Top 10's, it's great.

00:05:19.194 --> 00:05:21.527
We do one, five days a week.

00:05:23.034 --> 00:05:24.977
Like, we're workin' on new
Top 10 five days a week,

00:05:24.977 --> 00:05:26.882
we've been doing it for,
I don't know how long,

00:05:26.882 --> 00:05:29.023
I don't know how many Top
10's we have, there's a lot.

00:05:29.023 --> 00:05:31.537
If you want to come
into this market, right,

00:05:31.537 --> 00:05:34.531
if you want to now
compete, a lot of people,

00:05:34.531 --> 00:05:36.627
all they do is, they
make, like, one a month,

00:05:36.627 --> 00:05:38.380
two a month.

00:05:38.380 --> 00:05:39.881
And they're tryin' to
make it and they're tryin'

00:05:39.881 --> 00:05:41.252
to do better, but it's,
like, this start and stop,

00:05:41.252 --> 00:05:42.087
and start and stop.

00:05:42.087 --> 00:05:45.455
You're never going to win by
making that level of quantity

00:05:45.455 --> 00:05:47.344
'cause the quantity leads to the quality.

00:05:47.344 --> 00:05:49.341
The more you do, the
better it's going to get,

00:05:49.341 --> 00:05:51.600
and the more you'll find
your own flavor and style.

00:05:51.600 --> 00:05:53.886
You'll find a way to make
your own top 10 that's

00:05:53.886 --> 00:05:56.479
different than mine, on
it's own path, right?

00:05:56.479 --> 00:05:59.119
Like, you can't just be a
carbon copy of somebody else.

00:05:59.119 --> 00:06:01.016
You can start by copying,
but then you need to find

00:06:01.016 --> 00:06:03.589
your own twist and maneuvers,
and make it better.

00:06:03.589 --> 00:06:07.368
But it's not going to happen
doin' it once or twice a month,

00:06:07.368 --> 00:06:10.803
when people in your industry
are doing it everyday.

00:06:10.803 --> 00:06:11.636
Right?

00:06:11.636 --> 00:06:13.363
You need to get better.

00:06:13.363 --> 00:06:15.182
You need to get mastery.

00:06:15.182 --> 00:06:17.933
And so, one of the hardest
things for a lot of entrepreneurs

00:06:17.933 --> 00:06:21.287
to do is to focus,
because it's so exciting

00:06:21.287 --> 00:06:24.424
and it's so fun to work on
lots of different ideas, right?

00:06:24.424 --> 00:06:26.521
If you're an ideas person, it's just,

00:06:26.521 --> 00:06:29.183
"Oh, this is great and this
is great, oh, McDonald's could

00:06:29.183 --> 00:06:31.354
"really use this and
Staples could use this."

00:06:31.354 --> 00:06:33.746
And, I can walk into any
business and think of ways

00:06:33.746 --> 00:06:35.455
that they could make things better.

00:06:35.455 --> 00:06:37.705
And that's exciting, and
that's a great brain exercise,

00:06:37.705 --> 00:06:39.772
but you don't actually
get great at anything 'til

00:06:39.772 --> 00:06:42.301
you start executing, you start doing.

00:06:42.301 --> 00:06:44.706
And so, being able to take those ideas

00:06:44.706 --> 00:06:48.000
and then focus to say, "Of
all these things, this is what

00:06:48.000 --> 00:06:49.690
"I want to get really great at.

00:06:49.690 --> 00:06:51.814
"This is the skill that
want to learn to be one

00:06:51.814 --> 00:06:55.300
"of the best in the world
that's providing insane value

00:06:55.300 --> 00:06:57.431
"to my customers, to my market.

00:06:57.431 --> 00:07:00.248
"Outpace my competition, make
a ton of money in the process,

00:07:00.248 --> 00:07:01.707
"have a huge impact on the world.

00:07:01.707 --> 00:07:03.739
"This is what I want to do."

00:07:03.739 --> 00:07:07.810
It's the people who are
masters at their skill set

00:07:07.810 --> 00:07:11.324
who end up making the big
impact, making the big dollars,

00:07:11.324 --> 00:07:12.945
making the big changes.

00:07:12.945 --> 00:07:15.937
And the people who are
average or just above average,

00:07:15.937 --> 00:07:17.913
constantly live a small life.

00:07:17.913 --> 00:07:21.181
And so, you have to make a
choice, what do you want to do?

00:07:21.181 --> 00:07:23.329
Do you want to start and
stop, and start and stop,

00:07:23.329 --> 00:07:25.620
and never get good at anything
and bounce from one idea

00:07:25.620 --> 00:07:26.453
to the other?

00:07:26.453 --> 00:07:28.764
And, even if you have one
great idea, to never really,

00:07:28.764 --> 00:07:32.597
fully commit to it and
then, 10 years blow by,

00:07:33.935 --> 00:07:35.796
and nothing major has happened.

00:07:35.796 --> 00:07:37.598
'Cause you've never actually
committed serious time

00:07:37.598 --> 00:07:38.521
to doing it.

00:07:38.521 --> 00:07:41.877
Or do you want to say,
"No, this is important.

00:07:41.877 --> 00:07:45.022
"My time is important,
this project's important,

00:07:45.022 --> 00:07:47.380
"the impact that this is
going to have is important.

00:07:47.380 --> 00:07:49.615
"The result that it's
going to create for myself,

00:07:49.615 --> 00:07:52.076
"for my family, for my community,
for the people around me,

00:07:52.076 --> 00:07:52.969
"is important.

00:07:52.969 --> 00:07:56.344
"And I'm going to dedicate my
self to becoming a master at it."

00:07:56.344 --> 00:07:59.808
Stop tryin' to be okay
at a thousand things

00:07:59.808 --> 00:08:01.662
and master the one thing that you could be

00:08:01.662 --> 00:08:02.921
Tony Robbins level at.

00:08:02.921 --> 00:08:05.799
Do that and start having
tremendous success.

00:08:05.799 --> 00:08:07.014
So, the question of the day, today, is

00:08:07.014 --> 00:08:08.273
"What do you want to master?"

00:08:08.273 --> 00:08:11.078
What is the big thing
that you want to master,

00:08:11.078 --> 00:08:13.632
it's your big goal, you
want to be one of the best

00:08:13.632 --> 00:08:14.785
in the world at this thing.

00:08:14.785 --> 00:08:15.745
What is it?

00:08:15.745 --> 00:08:17.610
I'm curious, leave it
on the comments below.

00:08:17.610 --> 00:08:19.093
I want to see your answers.

00:08:19.093 --> 00:08:21.034
I also want to give a quick
shout out to Tim Hoven,

00:08:21.034 --> 00:08:22.227
from Hoven Farms.

00:08:22.227 --> 00:08:24.627
Tim thank you so much for
picking up a copy of my book,

00:08:24.627 --> 00:08:26.573
Your One Word, it really means a lot to me

00:08:26.573 --> 00:08:28.546
and I hope you're enjoying the read.

00:08:28.546 --> 00:08:29.927
So, thank you guys again for watching.

00:08:29.927 --> 00:08:32.009
I believe in you, I hope you continue

00:08:32.009 --> 00:08:34.922
to believe in yourself and
whatever your one word is.

00:08:34.922 --> 00:08:37.675
Much love and I'll see you
guys again tomorrow morning

00:08:37.675 --> 00:08:40.309
for another shot of Entspresso.

00:08:40.309 --> 00:08:43.152
♫ I wake up every mornin'

00:08:43.152 --> 00:08:46.652
♫ Espresso keep me goin'

00:08:49.797 --> 00:08:53.821
- I came to the mindset
when I was in film school,

00:08:53.821 --> 00:08:57.184
Mark, that I did not want
to be a one-and-done,

00:08:57.184 --> 00:09:00.369
I did not want to be a flash-in-the-pan.

00:09:00.369 --> 00:09:02.575
I wanted to be here for the long haul.

00:09:02.575 --> 00:09:04.976
When Kurasowa, the great Akira Kurasowa,

00:09:04.976 --> 00:09:09.188
was doing press for one
of his last films, Ran,

00:09:09.188 --> 00:09:13.373
his journalists asked him,
"You're one of the greatest

00:09:13.373 --> 00:09:15.580
"master filmmakers of all time,

00:09:15.580 --> 00:09:18.350
"what else do you still have to learn?"

00:09:18.350 --> 00:09:22.228
And Kurosawa said, "I still
have the universe to learn."

00:09:22.228 --> 00:09:26.292
So, I was in film school
and that blew my mind.

00:09:26.292 --> 00:09:30.884
The baddest motherfucka
ever, he's 85, 83, 84,

00:09:30.884 --> 00:09:32.932
and he says he still has
the universe to learn

00:09:32.932 --> 00:09:34.709
as far as storytelling?

00:09:34.709 --> 00:09:37.169
That really hit me, like,
you know, you really got to,

00:09:37.169 --> 00:09:39.798
like the jazz term, you got
to stay in the woodshed.

00:09:39.798 --> 00:09:42.423
You know, you got to shed,
you know, and constantly

00:09:42.423 --> 00:09:47.125
be on it, perfectin' your craft
and never get to the point

00:09:47.125 --> 00:09:51.002
that you trick yourself into
thinkin' that you know it all.

00:09:51.002 --> 00:09:53.919
It's a life-long journey, you know,

00:09:55.835 --> 00:09:58.923
to tell stories, how you craft them.

00:09:58.923 --> 00:10:01.729
Just be a good storyteller,
and you do that by,

00:10:01.729 --> 00:10:04.641
having a body of work, so it's always been

00:10:04.641 --> 00:10:06.321
on the body of work.

00:10:06.321 --> 00:10:09.418
- In everything I do, everyday,
everyday we're going down

00:10:09.418 --> 00:10:11.733
and working on the play, the whole thing.

00:10:11.733 --> 00:10:14.541
Everything is to get to a point where

00:10:14.541 --> 00:10:16.624
you don't have to act it.

00:10:18.104 --> 00:10:21.832
That all you're doing
is going through thing

00:10:21.832 --> 00:10:23.915
and you're not acting it.

00:10:24.945 --> 00:10:27.371
You just know what you're
talkin' about so much that

00:10:27.371 --> 00:10:29.454
you don't have to act it.

00:10:30.387 --> 00:10:33.897
You know what you're saying,
you know why you're saying it,

00:10:33.897 --> 00:10:37.026
and what you're talkin' about,

00:10:37.026 --> 00:10:38.153
you know who you're talkin' about,

00:10:38.153 --> 00:10:40.528
and how you feel when you talk about it,

00:10:40.528 --> 00:10:42.299
then you don't have to act it.

00:10:42.299 --> 00:10:45.294
And that's what the whole thing is about.

00:10:45.294 --> 00:10:48.652
- And by the time I got to
college, I made this album

00:10:48.652 --> 00:10:50.306
called The Language of My World.

00:10:50.306 --> 00:10:52.556
(cheering)

00:10:54.249 --> 00:10:57.183
And with this album, I did the same thing,

00:10:57.183 --> 00:11:00.334
I locked myself in my room
for, like, all of college.

00:11:00.334 --> 00:11:04.346
That was it, I just worked
my ass off, in my room.

00:11:04.346 --> 00:11:07.067
And kept writing, kept
recording, and I wasn't great

00:11:07.067 --> 00:11:09.572
going into college, but
by the time I got out,

00:11:09.572 --> 00:11:13.739
I had, kind of, like, learned
a little through the cracks.

00:11:15.257 --> 00:11:17.397
And by the time, when
I got out of college,

00:11:17.397 --> 00:11:21.564
I read this book called
Outliers by Malcolm Gladwell.

00:11:23.362 --> 00:11:24.396
Nobody's read it here.

00:11:24.396 --> 00:11:26.312
(cheering)

00:11:26.312 --> 00:11:27.229
One person.

00:11:28.092 --> 00:11:30.897
Anyway, this book examines...

00:11:30.897 --> 00:11:33.963
This book examines this theory, right?

00:11:33.963 --> 00:11:35.748
And Malcolm Gladwell
makes this point, he says,

00:11:35.748 --> 00:11:37.387
"It's not about what your
born with, it's not about

00:11:37.387 --> 00:11:40.249
"the talent that your born
with, it's about the hours

00:11:40.249 --> 00:11:43.001
"that you put into that talent."

00:11:43.001 --> 00:11:45.794
And he says, "If you want to
be a master of whatever it is

00:11:45.794 --> 00:11:48.393
"that you love to do, if you
want to develop that craft

00:11:48.393 --> 00:11:51.574
"to it's utmost potential,
what you got to do is work.

00:11:51.574 --> 00:11:53.885
"You have to work a lot,
you have to put in hours."

00:11:53.885 --> 00:11:57.968
And he says, "That if you
put in around 10,000 hours

00:11:57.968 --> 00:12:01.746
"into that one thing, that
eventually, you will master

00:12:01.746 --> 00:12:02.829
"that craft."

00:12:04.864 --> 00:12:06.863
And I don't know how close
I am to 10,000 hours,

00:12:06.863 --> 00:12:09.580
but every single time we step on a stage,

00:12:09.580 --> 00:12:11.497
we get one step closer.

